1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,964
[SQUEAKING]

2
00:00:01,964 --> 00:00:03,928
[RUSTLING]

3
00:00:03,928 --> 00:00:06,383
[CLICKING]

4
00:00:10,320 --> 00:00:12,160
TOBIAS COLDING: Last
time, we talked about--

5
00:00:12,160 --> 00:00:16,120
one of the things we talked
about last time was series.

6
00:00:16,120 --> 00:00:19,080
So we'll continue on that today.

7
00:00:19,080 --> 00:00:22,127
So series is that
you have a sequence.

8
00:00:22,127 --> 00:00:23,460
You're starting with a sequence.

9
00:00:23,460 --> 00:00:24,810
So a n is a sequence.

10
00:00:28,600 --> 00:00:31,020
And then you form
a new sequence.

11
00:00:31,020 --> 00:00:32,350
So you form a new sequence.

12
00:00:35,320 --> 00:00:45,030
A new sequence from
a n as follows.

13
00:00:51,400 --> 00:01:00,060
So the new sequence, let's call
that Sn, and S1 is just a1,

14
00:01:00,060 --> 00:01:14,940
S2 is a1 plus a2, S3 is a1
plus a2 plus a3, and so on.

15
00:01:14,940 --> 00:01:21,720
So this is the sum of
the first n elements.

16
00:01:24,740 --> 00:01:29,890
And you typically write a Sn.

17
00:01:32,820 --> 00:01:37,200
You write Sn as
the sum here from--

18
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:39,760
and then, in this case,
I'm starting at 1.

19
00:01:39,760 --> 00:01:47,740
But you could start at something
else, i equal to 1, to n of ai.

20
00:01:47,740 --> 00:01:50,700
And so to say that's the--

21
00:01:50,700 --> 00:01:52,420
so and you write the series.

22
00:01:52,420 --> 00:01:56,020
You also write-- so this is
slight abuse of notation,

23
00:01:56,020 --> 00:02:06,430
that you write this infinite
sum, sorry, as the series.

24
00:02:06,430 --> 00:02:08,229
So you think about this as--

25
00:02:08,229 --> 00:02:09,729
you think about this thing here.

26
00:02:09,729 --> 00:02:12,070
When you write this
thing here, you really

27
00:02:12,070 --> 00:02:16,150
think about the sequence Sn.

28
00:02:16,150 --> 00:02:27,750
So write this for
the sequence Sn.

29
00:02:27,750 --> 00:02:32,720
And so the series converge.

30
00:02:40,030 --> 00:02:52,730
This means that the
sequence Sn converges.

31
00:02:57,450 --> 00:03:00,910
And so the most--

32
00:03:00,910 --> 00:03:04,890
so remember that a sequence
converges-- so recall,

33
00:03:04,890 --> 00:03:09,010
by the Cauchy
convergence theorem--

34
00:03:09,010 --> 00:03:25,690
so recall by the Cauchy
convergence theorem

35
00:03:25,690 --> 00:03:42,980
that Sn converge if and only
if Sn is a Cauchy sequence.

36
00:03:51,010 --> 00:03:57,190
And a Cauchy sequence just means
that if you go sufficiently far

37
00:03:57,190 --> 00:04:00,430
out and you're looking
at this difference here,

38
00:04:00,430 --> 00:04:07,110
then this here is
small, provided

39
00:04:07,110 --> 00:04:11,230
that m and n are larger
than some capital

40
00:04:11,230 --> 00:04:17,003
N, where this here is
chosen depending on epsilon.

41
00:04:17,003 --> 00:04:20,269
That's what it means to
be a Cauchy sequence.

42
00:04:20,269 --> 00:04:30,790
So in particular, of course,
if you take-- so in particular,

43
00:04:30,790 --> 00:04:37,020
if you take m to be n plus 1--

44
00:04:39,650 --> 00:04:40,150
sorry.

45
00:04:40,150 --> 00:04:46,210
Let me say m to be n minus 1.

46
00:04:46,210 --> 00:04:47,950
Doesn't matter.

47
00:04:47,950 --> 00:04:54,520
So if I take it like
this, then Sn minus Sm.

48
00:04:54,520 --> 00:05:00,560
Well, this here is some
of the first n elements,

49
00:05:00,560 --> 00:05:03,360
and from that we
have to subtract

50
00:05:03,360 --> 00:05:09,160
the sum of the first
n minus 1 elements.

51
00:05:09,160 --> 00:05:11,100
So this here-- just
leave this here.

52
00:05:11,100 --> 00:05:13,520
Cancel out all of these.

53
00:05:13,520 --> 00:05:14,990
And so you're just
left with a n.

54
00:05:17,600 --> 00:05:24,920
So this means that if it is
converging, then in particular,

55
00:05:24,920 --> 00:05:32,960
a n here has to go to 0
as n goes to infinity.

56
00:05:32,960 --> 00:05:35,720
Because in fact,
this whole difference

57
00:05:35,720 --> 00:05:39,520
here, as long as m and n
are sufficiently large,

58
00:05:39,520 --> 00:05:43,320
has to be as small as you want.

59
00:05:43,320 --> 00:05:46,600
And so in a particularly
special case

60
00:05:46,600 --> 00:05:49,960
where this here is
the one prior to that,

61
00:05:49,960 --> 00:05:53,380
then you see that the a
n will have to go to 0.

62
00:05:53,380 --> 00:05:56,820
So again, if you see a
series, the first thing--

63
00:05:56,820 --> 00:05:58,440
you do don't apply any tests.

64
00:05:58,440 --> 00:06:02,540
The first thing that you check
is that this element here

65
00:06:02,540 --> 00:06:05,360
goes to 0.

66
00:06:05,360 --> 00:06:09,390
Now what are the most
important series?

67
00:06:12,060 --> 00:06:15,690
So the most important
is the geometric series.

68
00:06:20,980 --> 00:06:23,020
And the reason it's
the most important

69
00:06:23,020 --> 00:06:25,580
is that it can be
used to determine--

70
00:06:25,580 --> 00:06:29,100
it's easy to determine whether
it's converging or not.

71
00:06:29,100 --> 00:06:34,020
And it can be used to
determine using the test

72
00:06:34,020 --> 00:06:36,300
whether other series converge.

73
00:06:36,300 --> 00:06:40,380
And so the geometric series
is that you take a sum.

74
00:06:40,380 --> 00:06:43,400
Typically, here, you start at 0.

75
00:06:43,400 --> 00:06:45,100
You don't have to start at 0.

76
00:06:45,100 --> 00:06:47,680
But of course, the limit.

77
00:06:47,680 --> 00:06:48,180
The limit.

78
00:06:48,180 --> 00:06:52,320
If you take a series, then,
to say that it's converging,

79
00:06:52,320 --> 00:06:55,520
meaning that the
sequence Sn converges,

80
00:06:55,520 --> 00:07:00,580
and the limit of
these here is also.

81
00:07:00,580 --> 00:07:03,320
And that's where there's
this abuse of notation.

82
00:07:03,320 --> 00:07:05,380
It's denoted by the same thing.

83
00:07:05,380 --> 00:07:11,640
So this thing here is playing
the role of the sequence,

84
00:07:11,640 --> 00:07:15,160
but it's also
denoting the limit.

85
00:07:15,160 --> 00:07:18,000
That's just a standard thing.

86
00:07:18,000 --> 00:07:20,030
So in this case, I
was starting from 1.

87
00:07:26,080 --> 00:07:29,360
So now your matrix
series is where

88
00:07:29,360 --> 00:07:34,440
you take some number, c, and
then you multiply it by itself.

89
00:07:34,440 --> 00:07:40,100
This is some real number, and
you multiply it with itself

90
00:07:40,100 --> 00:07:44,840
i times, where i runs
from 0 to infinity.

91
00:07:44,840 --> 00:07:48,230
And when i is zero,
this is meant to be 1.

92
00:07:51,150 --> 00:07:54,450
And the reason why I
want to do it here at 0

93
00:07:54,450 --> 00:08:02,050
is that it's probably worth
remembering that this here, it's

94
00:08:02,050 --> 00:08:03,690
easy to deduce.

95
00:08:03,690 --> 00:08:10,550
But it's such a standard fact
that this here is converging.

96
00:08:13,290 --> 00:08:15,610
So the geometric
series converges,

97
00:08:15,610 --> 00:08:20,490
provided that the absolute
value of c is less than 1.

98
00:08:20,490 --> 00:08:23,300
And when it is, this
here is the limit.

99
00:08:26,130 --> 00:08:33,750
So then you can
ask if c here is 1,

100
00:08:33,750 --> 00:08:36,130
so if c is actually equal to 1.

101
00:08:36,130 --> 00:08:39,929
Not just the absolute value,
but if c is actually equal to 1,

102
00:08:39,929 --> 00:08:42,710
then it's just 1 times
itself a number of times.

103
00:08:42,710 --> 00:08:45,250
But that does not go to 0.

104
00:08:45,250 --> 00:08:47,650
So that's clearly diverging.

105
00:08:47,650 --> 00:08:51,570
And if it's minus 1 multiply
itself by a number of times,

106
00:08:51,570 --> 00:08:54,350
then it just alternates
between 1 and negative 1.

107
00:08:54,350 --> 00:08:55,870
So it doesn't go to 0.

108
00:08:55,870 --> 00:08:59,430
So it's divergent here.

109
00:08:59,430 --> 00:09:04,510
And in fact, as long as c
here is bigger or equal to--

110
00:09:04,510 --> 00:09:07,590
the absolute value is
bigger or equal to 1,

111
00:09:07,590 --> 00:09:10,190
because you take this
number and multiply it

112
00:09:10,190 --> 00:09:14,550
by itself a number of times,
if the absolute value of c

113
00:09:14,550 --> 00:09:17,930
is bigger than 1, then it
actually is even blowing up.

114
00:09:17,930 --> 00:09:22,510
It becomes larger and
larger in absolute value.

115
00:09:22,510 --> 00:09:26,210
So it's divergent here
and it's convergent.

116
00:09:26,210 --> 00:09:28,810
So that was kind of easy.

117
00:09:28,810 --> 00:09:32,270
And it was easy to
determine why we did--

118
00:09:32,270 --> 00:09:38,230
this little trick will
determine what this limit was.

119
00:09:38,230 --> 00:09:41,350
And this is a trick that
goes back, I think, certainly

120
00:09:41,350 --> 00:09:44,950
to the ancient Greeks,
I mean, this idea.

121
00:09:44,950 --> 00:09:47,850
And you can do this with various
other things, these things

122
00:09:47,850 --> 00:09:51,630
about adding things in
different order kind of stuff,

123
00:09:51,630 --> 00:09:55,510
or multiplying by something.

124
00:09:55,510 --> 00:09:57,150
So that was the most important.

125
00:09:57,150 --> 00:10:00,030
And again, we'll come back to
why it is the most important,

126
00:10:00,030 --> 00:10:03,410
but it's because it can
be used to determine

127
00:10:03,410 --> 00:10:06,610
whether other series
converge or not.

128
00:10:06,610 --> 00:10:17,090
Another classical, but that
sits inside a family of series

129
00:10:17,090 --> 00:10:19,730
is the harmonic series.

130
00:10:19,730 --> 00:10:23,190
So this is an
example of a series.

131
00:10:23,190 --> 00:10:30,770
It's one series that sits inside
a larger family of series.

132
00:10:30,770 --> 00:10:35,170
And so the harmonic series
is when you take 1 over n

133
00:10:35,170 --> 00:10:38,890
and you're summing
here like that.

134
00:10:38,890 --> 00:10:44,470
This series here, and we saw
that last time, is divergent.

135
00:10:47,620 --> 00:10:51,900
And in fact, the larger series
that is sitting inside is the--

136
00:10:51,900 --> 00:10:58,270
sits inside a larger family.

137
00:11:03,220 --> 00:11:06,140
And that larger
family is where you're

138
00:11:06,140 --> 00:11:11,940
summing from 1 to infinity
of 1 over n to the alpha.

139
00:11:11,940 --> 00:11:16,860
And here, alpha is taken
to be bigger or equal to 1.

140
00:11:16,860 --> 00:11:19,580
And so in the case where
alpha is equal to 1,

141
00:11:19,580 --> 00:11:21,280
that's a harmonic series.

142
00:11:21,280 --> 00:11:23,020
These ones diverge.

143
00:11:23,020 --> 00:11:27,460
But if alpha-- and so this
here is for alpha equal to 1.

144
00:11:27,460 --> 00:11:29,690
It's diverged because
it's the harmonic series.

145
00:11:32,580 --> 00:11:36,020
And when alpha is strictly
bigger than 1, what we

146
00:11:36,020 --> 00:11:38,220
just-- last time, we
just saw one example

147
00:11:38,220 --> 00:11:41,260
of this when alpha
was equal to 2,

148
00:11:41,260 --> 00:11:43,080
and we saw that that converged.

149
00:11:43,080 --> 00:11:47,520
But in fact, you can prove that
for any alpha strictly bigger

150
00:11:47,520 --> 00:11:49,910
than 1, it does converge.

151
00:11:53,600 --> 00:11:57,640
And the typical thing
to use there is--

152
00:11:57,640 --> 00:12:00,400
and we'll talk about that
at some later lecture

153
00:12:00,400 --> 00:12:02,210
is what's called
the integral test.

154
00:12:09,680 --> 00:12:22,470
Now there is-- when you have a
series, then most, but not all--

155
00:12:25,280 --> 00:12:30,800
most tests, but again,
not all, determine really

156
00:12:30,800 --> 00:12:35,520
whether the series is what's
called absolutely convergent.

157
00:12:35,520 --> 00:12:36,968
So what is absolutely
convergent?

158
00:12:36,968 --> 00:12:38,260
We talked about that last time.

159
00:12:38,260 --> 00:12:41,480
But let me remind you.

160
00:12:41,480 --> 00:12:44,180
Absolute convergence.

161
00:12:48,900 --> 00:12:56,100
So if you take a
series here, then here

162
00:12:56,100 --> 00:12:59,100
is absolutely convergent.

163
00:12:59,100 --> 00:13:00,880
Is absolutely convergent.

164
00:13:09,140 --> 00:13:17,380
This just means-- it means that
the series where you're slapping

165
00:13:17,380 --> 00:13:22,180
on absolute value,
so this thing here,

166
00:13:22,180 --> 00:13:24,520
means that this series
here is convergent.

167
00:13:29,980 --> 00:13:32,360
And so it's easy to see.

168
00:13:32,360 --> 00:13:37,700
So easy to see.

169
00:13:37,700 --> 00:13:41,020
And we saw this last time.

170
00:13:41,020 --> 00:13:43,610
And it was just from the
Cauchy convergence theorem.

171
00:13:52,350 --> 00:14:05,750
It is easy to see that absolute
convergence implies convergence,

172
00:14:05,750 --> 00:14:07,750
but not the other way around.

173
00:14:07,750 --> 00:14:12,430
So Cauchy convergence theorem
gives you this implication.

174
00:14:12,430 --> 00:14:17,350
Why it's not the case that
you have the other way around?

175
00:14:17,350 --> 00:14:25,670
And that is because what's
called the alternating series.

176
00:14:25,670 --> 00:14:29,550
So the alternating series.

177
00:14:29,550 --> 00:14:30,570
Alternating.

178
00:14:34,550 --> 00:14:38,270
And there's something called
the alternating series test.

179
00:14:38,270 --> 00:14:41,810
But let me just
explain to you in words

180
00:14:41,810 --> 00:14:44,530
why the alternating
series converge.

181
00:14:44,530 --> 00:14:46,580
So the alternating
harmonic series.

182
00:14:54,330 --> 00:15:01,330
So if you take this series
here minus 1 to the n times--

183
00:15:01,330 --> 00:15:07,410
over n and you sum
here from 0 to--

184
00:15:07,410 --> 00:15:11,490
sorry, not 0, because you're
dividing by 0, so from 1

185
00:15:11,490 --> 00:15:16,090
to infinity, this here is
called the alternating series.

186
00:15:16,090 --> 00:15:18,930
Alternating harmonic series.

187
00:15:18,930 --> 00:15:22,290
If you were slapping an
absolute value sign on it,

188
00:15:22,290 --> 00:15:28,850
this means that a n here is
minus 1 to the power n over n.

189
00:15:28,850 --> 00:15:34,930
So if you take the
absolute value sign of a n,

190
00:15:34,930 --> 00:15:37,410
then this here is just 1.

191
00:15:37,410 --> 00:15:39,370
So this is 1 over n.

192
00:15:39,370 --> 00:15:43,270
So this here is the
corresponding series when

193
00:15:43,270 --> 00:15:45,070
you're slapping absolute value.

194
00:15:45,070 --> 00:15:46,640
And this is the harmonic series.

195
00:15:51,910 --> 00:15:55,170
And this is divergent.

196
00:15:55,170 --> 00:15:57,830
That was what we just discussed.

197
00:15:57,830 --> 00:16:00,722
Now why is this series
here convergent?

198
00:16:00,722 --> 00:16:04,590
We'll probably come back to that
in a later lecture in a more

199
00:16:04,590 --> 00:16:08,060
general setting, but let
me just explain this.

200
00:16:11,910 --> 00:16:21,030
And so the idea is that
before you start summing,

201
00:16:21,030 --> 00:16:25,030
imagine that you have here--
before you start your sum,

202
00:16:25,030 --> 00:16:28,642
then you are in a neutral
position, which is 1.

203
00:16:28,642 --> 00:16:30,030
0, sorry.

204
00:16:30,030 --> 00:16:35,350
And then you do the first thing.

205
00:16:35,350 --> 00:16:38,210
You're looking at S1.

206
00:16:38,210 --> 00:16:43,280
But S1 will bring
you down, because S1

207
00:16:43,280 --> 00:16:47,740
is just equal to minus
1 to the power 1.

208
00:16:47,740 --> 00:16:49,060
But that's minus 1.

209
00:16:49,060 --> 00:16:51,280
So it's minus 1 over n.

210
00:16:51,280 --> 00:16:53,800
This is the first element.

211
00:16:53,800 --> 00:16:56,040
So that's going
to bring you down.

212
00:16:56,040 --> 00:16:59,720
Let me just
exaggerate the picture

213
00:16:59,720 --> 00:17:04,359
and bring you down
here to minus 1 over n.

214
00:17:04,359 --> 00:17:08,180
Minus 1 over-- n is equal to 1.

215
00:17:08,180 --> 00:17:11,160
So this is minus 1.

216
00:17:11,160 --> 00:17:15,000
This is how the series starts.

217
00:17:15,000 --> 00:17:18,680
And so the next element is S2.

218
00:17:18,680 --> 00:17:21,020
But S2 is minus 1.

219
00:17:21,020 --> 00:17:22,760
That was the first element.

220
00:17:22,760 --> 00:17:30,480
And then plus and then
minus 1 squared over 2.

221
00:17:30,480 --> 00:17:33,920
So this is-- and you would
be tempted, but it's not--

222
00:17:33,920 --> 00:17:36,300
don't add them at this stage.

223
00:17:36,300 --> 00:17:40,610
But just looking
at it like this.

224
00:17:40,610 --> 00:17:48,460
So the next-- so
this here is S1.

225
00:17:48,460 --> 00:17:50,700
Let me just write it down here.

226
00:17:50,700 --> 00:17:52,580
This is S1.

227
00:17:52,580 --> 00:17:57,780
When you're looking at S2, then
you're coming back towards 0.

228
00:17:57,780 --> 00:18:02,440
But you don't go as far
up as you went down,

229
00:18:02,440 --> 00:18:05,580
because it's 1 over 2.

230
00:18:05,580 --> 00:18:06,080
Right.

231
00:18:06,080 --> 00:18:08,740
So this here is S2.

232
00:18:08,740 --> 00:18:12,300
And then you see
the next one is--

233
00:18:12,300 --> 00:18:13,360
the next one.

234
00:18:13,360 --> 00:18:18,500
If you start here, then you
are supposed to-- so for S3,

235
00:18:18,500 --> 00:18:26,220
it's really S2 plus minus
1 to the power 3 over 3.

236
00:18:26,220 --> 00:18:32,100
So this is S2 minus, because
minus 1 to the power 3

237
00:18:32,100 --> 00:18:35,400
is a negative sign like that.

238
00:18:35,400 --> 00:18:38,800
So the next one here--

239
00:18:38,800 --> 00:18:42,460
so you're starting here before
you have done any summation.

240
00:18:42,460 --> 00:18:44,000
You're starting at 0.

241
00:18:44,000 --> 00:18:45,440
You're brought down here.

242
00:18:45,440 --> 00:18:47,960
You come up here.

243
00:18:47,960 --> 00:18:50,300
But then in S3,
you're going down,

244
00:18:50,300 --> 00:18:53,600
but you don't go as far
down as the previous one.

245
00:18:53,600 --> 00:18:55,320
So you're going down here.

246
00:18:55,320 --> 00:18:58,220
And so you're really
continuing this process.

247
00:18:58,220 --> 00:19:01,000
And you see that this interval
gets smaller and smaller,

248
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:03,380
and it just goes down,
and then it come up.

249
00:19:03,380 --> 00:19:07,200
But it doesn't go as far
up as you were before,

250
00:19:07,200 --> 00:19:08,700
and then so on and so forth.

251
00:19:08,700 --> 00:19:11,500
And so you could make
this more precise.

252
00:19:11,500 --> 00:19:13,000
It's quite easy.

253
00:19:13,000 --> 00:19:16,460
And this shows you that
this series is converging.

254
00:19:16,460 --> 00:19:20,680
And in fact, this is the basis
for this alternating series

255
00:19:20,680 --> 00:19:21,180
test.

256
00:19:21,180 --> 00:19:24,440
And it works as long as
you have alternating--

257
00:19:24,440 --> 00:19:27,800
where the sign of the
a n's are alternating

258
00:19:27,800 --> 00:19:30,240
and the absolute
value goes to 0.

259
00:19:30,240 --> 00:19:32,840
Then this here, that's
your alternating series.

260
00:19:32,840 --> 00:19:35,970
So I'll probably come back
to that at some later stage.

261
00:19:35,970 --> 00:19:44,050
But this just shows you that
if you take a series, then--

262
00:19:44,050 --> 00:19:46,770
So the absolute
convergence means

263
00:19:46,770 --> 00:19:48,990
that if you slap
absolute value sign,

264
00:19:48,990 --> 00:19:51,930
then that new series
also converges.

265
00:19:51,930 --> 00:19:55,065
Absolute convergence
implies convergence, but not

266
00:19:55,065 --> 00:19:55,940
the other way around.

267
00:19:58,750 --> 00:20:03,910
So now the next thing is that--

268
00:20:03,910 --> 00:20:07,200
so the next thing is that
if you take a series--

269
00:20:11,370 --> 00:20:16,730
suppose you take a series and
suppose that you knew that--

270
00:20:16,730 --> 00:20:24,610
so suppose you have a series
like this and you knew that

271
00:20:24,610 --> 00:20:26,470
a n here--

272
00:20:26,470 --> 00:20:29,750
all of the a n's were
non-negative for all n.

273
00:20:33,590 --> 00:20:35,950
Then convergence here.

274
00:20:35,950 --> 00:20:36,950
Convergence.

275
00:20:41,110 --> 00:20:42,370
Just implied, right?

276
00:20:42,370 --> 00:20:47,550
So convergence is-- because
the Sn is now the sum here

277
00:20:47,550 --> 00:20:48,890
of these guys here.

278
00:20:51,470 --> 00:20:53,830
That's Sn.

279
00:20:53,830 --> 00:20:55,850
And you see that
this is, of course,

280
00:20:55,850 --> 00:20:58,150
less than or equal to--
because all of these guys

281
00:20:58,150 --> 00:21:03,030
are non-negative, it's less than
or equal to where you summing.

282
00:21:03,030 --> 00:21:08,710
Not just up to n, but one more,
because this is non-negative.

283
00:21:08,710 --> 00:21:11,190
And so this is Sn plus 1.

284
00:21:11,190 --> 00:21:14,910
So you see that if the
elements are non-negative,

285
00:21:14,910 --> 00:21:18,610
then the sequence Sn is--

286
00:21:18,610 --> 00:21:24,210
so Sn is monotone
non-decreasing sequence.

287
00:21:24,210 --> 00:21:27,870
And so to say-- by the
monotone convergence theorem,

288
00:21:27,870 --> 00:21:31,510
to say that this sequence,
Sn, is convergent,

289
00:21:31,510 --> 00:21:33,950
is equivalent to saying that.

290
00:21:33,950 --> 00:21:47,500
So Sn converges if and only
if Sn is bounded from above.

291
00:21:58,290 --> 00:21:58,790
OK.

292
00:21:58,790 --> 00:22:03,890
So now when you have a
series and you have already

293
00:22:03,890 --> 00:22:08,730
checked that a n goes
to 0, and you are then

294
00:22:08,730 --> 00:22:13,590
asked to determine whether the
series is convergent or not,

295
00:22:13,590 --> 00:22:15,830
then there are these
number of tests.

296
00:22:15,830 --> 00:22:18,150
And so there's a bunch of tests.

297
00:22:18,150 --> 00:22:24,750
So tests for convergence.

298
00:22:30,090 --> 00:22:32,220
And so there's a bunch of them.

299
00:22:32,220 --> 00:22:35,340
And let me talk about--

300
00:22:35,340 --> 00:22:37,870
in this lecture, I'll just
talk about the comparison test.

301
00:22:40,740 --> 00:22:46,040
And I mentioned them
very briefly last time,

302
00:22:46,040 --> 00:22:48,020
but I'll talk a little
bit more about them.

303
00:22:48,020 --> 00:22:49,570
And then there's the ratio test.

304
00:22:56,980 --> 00:22:59,120
And then there's the root test.

305
00:22:59,120 --> 00:23:01,180
So I'll talk about those three.

306
00:23:01,180 --> 00:23:02,740
But there are other tests.

307
00:23:02,740 --> 00:23:03,590
So root test.

308
00:23:14,500 --> 00:23:20,140
So now let's start with
the comparison test.

309
00:23:20,140 --> 00:23:22,850
And there are two
versions of this.

310
00:23:27,060 --> 00:23:30,660
One version is the following.

311
00:23:30,660 --> 00:23:40,460
Let's suppose that you have--
so comparison test version one.

312
00:23:46,240 --> 00:23:49,640
And so the first version
of the comparison test

313
00:23:49,640 --> 00:23:50,780
is the following.

314
00:23:50,780 --> 00:23:56,460
Imagine that you have two
series here, a n, and again,

315
00:23:56,460 --> 00:23:58,220
we talked about this last time.

316
00:23:58,220 --> 00:24:02,880
But let me just say a few more
words, that you have this series

317
00:24:02,880 --> 00:24:04,840
and you have that series.

318
00:24:04,840 --> 00:24:11,080
And I'm assuming here
that a n and the bn

319
00:24:11,080 --> 00:24:12,540
are non-negative numbers.

320
00:24:12,540 --> 00:24:17,800
And in fact, I'm assuming that
bn is larger or equal to a n.

321
00:24:17,800 --> 00:24:20,460
In the comparison
test, this version,

322
00:24:20,460 --> 00:24:28,460
the simple version of the
comparison test is this here.

323
00:24:28,460 --> 00:24:38,780
If the series based on
the bn is convergent,

324
00:24:38,780 --> 00:24:47,160
then the series of the a
n's is also convergent.

325
00:24:52,860 --> 00:24:57,660
And so this version
of the comparison test

326
00:24:57,660 --> 00:25:00,820
is used both in this
way, but it's also

327
00:25:00,820 --> 00:25:05,720
used sometimes where you know
that this series is divergent.

328
00:25:05,720 --> 00:25:08,380
And from that, you conclude
that this one is just

329
00:25:08,380 --> 00:25:09,960
a negation of this statement.

330
00:25:14,420 --> 00:25:18,100
So let's see.

331
00:25:18,100 --> 00:25:24,900
So the proof here of
that is actually easy.

332
00:25:24,900 --> 00:25:28,390
So why is this the case?

333
00:25:28,390 --> 00:25:38,670
Well, the thing is that you
know by the Cauchy convergence

334
00:25:38,670 --> 00:25:39,170
theorem.

335
00:25:46,790 --> 00:25:51,790
And we know that to
determine whether--

336
00:25:51,790 --> 00:25:56,110
all we need to check to
determine whether this sequence

337
00:25:56,110 --> 00:25:57,870
here is convergent--

338
00:25:57,870 --> 00:26:08,230
all we need to figure out is
if it is a Cauchy sequence.

339
00:26:08,230 --> 00:26:12,310
So this means that
you want to say that--

340
00:26:12,310 --> 00:26:23,110
so suppose you're looking at
the sequence defined from a.

341
00:26:23,110 --> 00:26:28,010
And so this is just where
I'm now summing the first n

342
00:26:28,010 --> 00:26:32,250
elements in a.

343
00:26:32,250 --> 00:26:35,630
And I just want to--

344
00:26:35,630 --> 00:26:40,490
so I'm using the
superscript a just

345
00:26:40,490 --> 00:26:44,250
to denote that it's coming
from the sequence a n.

346
00:26:44,250 --> 00:26:48,670
So if you're looking at
this, and so likewise,

347
00:26:48,670 --> 00:26:50,450
I look at this thing here.

348
00:26:55,130 --> 00:26:58,210
Now, of course, you
have that suppose you're

349
00:26:58,210 --> 00:27:02,650
looking at Sna minus Sma.

350
00:27:05,570 --> 00:27:08,730
You want to show
that this here--

351
00:27:08,730 --> 00:27:11,630
you want to show that this
here is a Cauchy sequence.

352
00:27:11,630 --> 00:27:15,010
So you're looking at
this difference here.

353
00:27:15,010 --> 00:27:19,570
But this thing here is
just this here difference.

354
00:27:19,570 --> 00:27:23,810
I'm assuming that
m is less than n.

355
00:27:23,810 --> 00:27:25,710
Strictly less than m.

356
00:27:25,710 --> 00:27:27,030
n, sorry.

357
00:27:27,030 --> 00:27:30,510
This just means that
this difference here

358
00:27:30,510 --> 00:27:35,250
is just where you're
coming from m plus 1.

359
00:27:35,250 --> 00:27:42,416
So this is just the
a m plus 1 up to a n.

360
00:27:42,416 --> 00:27:45,630
This is just this one.

361
00:27:45,630 --> 00:27:46,710
Yeah?

362
00:27:46,710 --> 00:27:50,730
AUDIENCE: When we're proving
convergence, there's now--

363
00:27:50,730 --> 00:27:52,870
just to give some
motivation, is the reason

364
00:27:52,870 --> 00:27:55,613
that we're using
Cauchy sequences?

365
00:27:55,613 --> 00:27:57,030
TOBIAS COLDING:
At this stage, you

366
00:27:57,030 --> 00:27:59,770
could either use Cauchy
sequence, that's what I started.

367
00:27:59,770 --> 00:28:02,810
You could also have used the
monotone convergence theorem.

368
00:28:02,810 --> 00:28:04,394
Maybe that would be--

369
00:28:04,394 --> 00:28:04,936
AUDIENCE: OK.

370
00:28:04,936 --> 00:28:05,550
Because I was just
going to say, back

371
00:28:05,550 --> 00:28:07,633
to the definition of
convergence that we initially

372
00:28:07,633 --> 00:28:10,252
had where we had the absolute
value of a of n minus some--

373
00:28:10,252 --> 00:28:11,710
TOBIAS COLDING:
Yeah, that's right.

374
00:28:11,710 --> 00:28:13,690
AUDIENCE: Is it
just like-- is it--

375
00:28:13,690 --> 00:28:16,150
TOBIAS COLDING: So if
you went all the way back

376
00:28:16,150 --> 00:28:18,350
to the definition,
then you would

377
00:28:18,350 --> 00:28:20,640
have to determine
what the limit was.

378
00:28:20,640 --> 00:28:21,430
AUDIENCE: Right,
which we don't want.

379
00:28:21,430 --> 00:28:23,763
TOBIAS COLDING: And we don't
want because we don't know.

380
00:28:23,763 --> 00:28:26,160
But just because we may
know what the limit of b--

381
00:28:26,160 --> 00:28:28,980
of the sequence based on b is.

382
00:28:28,980 --> 00:28:31,160
But this doesn't mean that
you can figure out what

383
00:28:31,160 --> 00:28:32,120
the limit of the a--

384
00:28:32,120 --> 00:28:34,920
AUDIENCE: So does that
motivate why the sequences are

385
00:28:34,920 --> 00:28:35,940
more useful in general?

386
00:28:35,940 --> 00:28:36,580
TOBIAS COLDING:
Yeah, that's right.

387
00:28:36,580 --> 00:28:36,940
That's right.

388
00:28:36,940 --> 00:28:37,482
That's right.

389
00:28:37,482 --> 00:28:38,460
Exactly, that's right.

390
00:28:38,460 --> 00:28:39,002
That's right.

391
00:28:39,002 --> 00:28:41,080
So the advantage
with Cauchy sequence

392
00:28:41,080 --> 00:28:44,340
is that you don't have to
figure out what the limit is.

393
00:28:44,340 --> 00:28:48,060
And it's often-- I mean, like
in this case, you have no idea.

394
00:28:48,060 --> 00:28:51,980
I mean, it's not in terms
of anything from the b's.

395
00:28:51,980 --> 00:28:56,240
And I should-- but let me just
continue doing it like this.

396
00:28:56,240 --> 00:28:59,320
But just now when you
asked, I should probably

397
00:28:59,320 --> 00:29:02,880
have done it using the
monotone convergence theorem.

398
00:29:02,880 --> 00:29:04,700
But you can do that
as an exercise.

399
00:29:04,700 --> 00:29:07,240
But it doesn't matter.

400
00:29:07,240 --> 00:29:11,980
So the difference here is--

401
00:29:11,980 --> 00:29:14,320
this is what the difference is.

402
00:29:14,320 --> 00:29:17,040
The absolute value signs
are really irrelevant,

403
00:29:17,040 --> 00:29:19,140
because these are all
non-negative numbers.

404
00:29:19,140 --> 00:29:23,685
So I don't have to have
the absolute value sign.

405
00:29:23,685 --> 00:29:27,200
And now this thing here
is, of course, less than.

406
00:29:27,200 --> 00:29:29,950
But this here is less than
the corresponding b's.

407
00:29:34,420 --> 00:29:39,660
And this is less than the
corresponding b here like that.

408
00:29:39,660 --> 00:29:48,500
But this here is exactly
Sn, the one based on bSm.

409
00:29:48,500 --> 00:29:50,060
Based on b.

410
00:29:50,060 --> 00:29:53,760
And this here, as long as n
and m are sufficiently large,

411
00:29:53,760 --> 00:29:55,820
we can make this as
small as we want.

412
00:29:55,820 --> 00:29:58,150
So therefore, this here
was the smallest one.

413
00:30:01,700 --> 00:30:03,380
You could also have
done it-- maybe

414
00:30:03,380 --> 00:30:05,540
let's just say
that you could also

415
00:30:05,540 --> 00:30:10,040
have done it using the monotone
convergence, because now,

416
00:30:10,040 --> 00:30:15,060
of course, you have that.

417
00:30:15,060 --> 00:30:16,900
Again, the same of thing.

418
00:30:16,900 --> 00:30:21,780
Say that if you take
the Sn based on the a's.

419
00:30:21,780 --> 00:30:27,320
So that's a1 up to a
n sum of these guys.

420
00:30:27,320 --> 00:30:31,110
But this individually is less
than the corresponding b's.

421
00:30:34,560 --> 00:30:41,040
And this here is just the
sequence Sn based on the b's.

422
00:30:41,040 --> 00:30:45,360
But these here converge.

423
00:30:45,360 --> 00:30:48,480
This is equivalent to saying
that they are bounded.

424
00:30:48,480 --> 00:30:49,920
They are less than some c--

425
00:30:49,920 --> 00:30:51,920
some real number c.

426
00:30:51,920 --> 00:30:53,660
And this here is for all a.

427
00:30:57,040 --> 00:30:59,120
For all n, sorry.

428
00:30:59,120 --> 00:31:01,560
But this means, of course,
that those things here

429
00:31:01,560 --> 00:31:05,260
are bounded for all n.

430
00:31:05,260 --> 00:31:10,760
And this here is also a monotone
sequence-- monotone increasing

431
00:31:10,760 --> 00:31:13,880
sequence that is not
bounded from above.

432
00:31:13,880 --> 00:31:16,360
So therefore, it converges.

433
00:31:16,360 --> 00:31:19,690
So you can do it either
of those two ways.

434
00:31:19,690 --> 00:31:22,530
So that's really easy to check.

435
00:31:22,530 --> 00:31:26,340
Let's see an example where
we are using this test here.

436
00:31:34,490 --> 00:31:43,170
And so this could be that you're
looking at something like--

437
00:31:43,170 --> 00:31:45,880
you're looking at--
so here's an example.

438
00:31:50,530 --> 00:31:54,890
You're looking at a series
that is true to the minus n

439
00:31:54,890 --> 00:32:00,550
over n, n equal
to 1 to infinity.

440
00:32:04,050 --> 00:32:08,090
And you want to show--
you're being asked,

441
00:32:08,090 --> 00:32:14,210
is this series converging?

442
00:32:17,170 --> 00:32:22,750
So this is typically
how it works.

443
00:32:22,750 --> 00:32:24,950
And so, of course,
in this case, you

444
00:32:24,950 --> 00:32:30,830
should think about that
this here is a sequence a n.

445
00:32:30,830 --> 00:32:33,750
And so you have to find the bn.

446
00:32:33,750 --> 00:32:37,430
But in this case, we just
say-- so we're setting a.

447
00:32:37,430 --> 00:32:49,230
So answer set a n equal
to 2 to minus n over 2

448
00:32:49,230 --> 00:32:53,930
and bn equal to
2 to the minus n.

449
00:32:58,683 --> 00:33:05,760
Then you have that a n bigger or
equal to 0 is smaller than bn.

450
00:33:09,350 --> 00:33:17,130
And the sequence
here based on the bn

451
00:33:17,130 --> 00:33:23,520
is a convergent
geometric series.

452
00:33:28,970 --> 00:33:34,830
It's convergent between--
because c here, in this case,

453
00:33:34,830 --> 00:33:36,570
is 1 over 2.

454
00:33:36,570 --> 00:33:38,690
It's 1 over 2.

455
00:33:38,690 --> 00:33:41,170
So it's just 1 over 2.

456
00:33:41,170 --> 00:33:44,230
1.5 is the same as
2 to the minus 1.

457
00:33:47,450 --> 00:33:49,550
And so this is convergent.

458
00:33:52,910 --> 00:33:55,490
So this is just so--

459
00:33:55,490 --> 00:33:58,570
so typically, this could
be used to-- that you're

460
00:33:58,570 --> 00:34:00,690
given a sequence and
you're being asked,

461
00:34:00,690 --> 00:34:02,690
is convergent or not?

462
00:34:02,690 --> 00:34:06,170
And so you have to
find the sequence

463
00:34:06,170 --> 00:34:09,330
and you're being asked whether
it's convergent or not.

464
00:34:09,330 --> 00:34:14,489
And you have to find
the comparison series.

465
00:34:14,489 --> 00:34:24,460
And then, in this case, say that
this thing here is convergent.

466
00:34:24,460 --> 00:34:27,500
And so therefore,
the sequence a n

467
00:34:27,500 --> 00:34:34,460
that was squeezed between that
and 0 was also convergent.

468
00:34:34,460 --> 00:34:40,580
So this was the first version
of the comparison test.

469
00:34:40,580 --> 00:34:47,300
Here is another more fancy
version of comparing,

470
00:34:47,300 --> 00:34:50,089
where you're
comparing two series.

471
00:34:53,260 --> 00:34:57,500
In fact, it's pretty much like
that, except for this stuff

472
00:34:57,500 --> 00:34:59,540
we talked about.

473
00:34:59,540 --> 00:35:05,620
I mean, typically the tests
are where you're comparing it

474
00:35:05,620 --> 00:35:09,060
with some other series.

475
00:35:09,060 --> 00:35:11,580
So here is version two.

476
00:35:11,580 --> 00:35:19,500
So comparison version two.

477
00:35:26,225 --> 00:35:31,480
And so you have,
again, two series, a n.

478
00:35:31,480 --> 00:35:33,840
And again, it doesn't matter--

479
00:35:33,840 --> 00:35:36,580
what the sum is matters
where you're starting,

480
00:35:36,580 --> 00:35:41,320
but whether or not it converges
doesn't matter where you start.

481
00:35:41,320 --> 00:35:45,120
So suppose that you have this
series and you have this series

482
00:35:45,120 --> 00:35:45,620
here.

483
00:35:48,840 --> 00:36:00,200
And suppose that you know that
the none of the bn's are 0.

484
00:36:00,200 --> 00:36:06,160
And you know that the
a n divided by bn,

485
00:36:06,160 --> 00:36:11,080
that this ratio here,
as n goes to infinity,

486
00:36:11,080 --> 00:36:17,140
converges to some
L. And L here is--

487
00:36:17,140 --> 00:36:22,980
L here is not 0 and
is not infinity.

488
00:36:22,980 --> 00:36:29,060
I mean that it really
is a limit, like that.

489
00:36:29,060 --> 00:36:32,610
So if that's the case,
then the series--

490
00:36:39,380 --> 00:36:52,810
then let's-- then let's have
these as non-negative numbers.

491
00:37:01,660 --> 00:37:19,430
Then the series a n converges
if and only if the series bn

492
00:37:19,430 --> 00:37:20,250
converges.

493
00:37:30,270 --> 00:37:33,270
And so an example of this--

494
00:37:33,270 --> 00:37:36,790
so a typical example
of this is where

495
00:37:36,790 --> 00:37:45,110
you're looking at the series
1 over n squared minus 1.

496
00:37:45,110 --> 00:37:46,990
Suppose you're looking at this.

497
